Your rules for this feature, as the builders will follow them (each one applies to every case like it, not just the example):
  1. Whenever a customer asks for a refund on an order that is not damaged and is not final sale, accept it if the request is made up to 14 days after delivery, and refuse it after that. This applies to every item in the order.
  2. Whenever a refund request is evaluated, evaluate it once for the whole order, not item by item. If the order is marked final sale, give no refund for any item unless the order arrived damaged. If it is not final sale, apply the normal day-based refund rules to all items in the order.
  3. Whenever a refund request has a blank reason or a reason like "just because", treat it as a changed-mind refund under the normal changed-mind rules. Treat the item as damaged only when the stated reason is "damaged".
  4. Whenever an order arrives damaged, refund it in full, including shipping, up to 90 days. For example, an order of Rs 2,000 for the item plus Rs 150 shipping gets Rs 2,150 back.
  5. Whenever an order is refunded, the total refunded for that order is at most the amount the customer actually paid (after any coupon), and the order cannot be refunded a second time.
  6. Whenever a refund amount is not a whole rupee amount, round it down to the whole rupee, in the shop's favour. For example, Rs 1,249.60 becomes Rs 1,249.
  7. Whenever a refund is paid, pay it back the way the order was originally paid, and never swap methods. Card orders are refunded to the card and store credit orders are refunded as store credit. This also applies when the refund is for the wrong colour being sent by mistake.
  8. Whenever an order is refunded, the customer keeps the loyalty points the order earned. Refunds never take points away.
  9. Whenever an order is paid entirely by card, it earns 1 loyalty point for every full Rs 100 paid for the items after the discount code is applied (before shipping and tax). For example, Rs 1,250 paid by card on a non-final-sale order earns 12 points. An order under Rs 500 earns no points, and a final-sale order earns double. An order paid partly or entirely with store credit earns no points.
  10. Whenever the daily report is produced, "Orders" is the number of every order in the list, whatever its status (cancelled, unpaid or refunded orders count like any other). "Revenue" is the sum of the totals (items after discount plus shipping) of card orders only, with no refunds subtracted. Store-credit orders add nothing to revenue. When there are no orders, the report shows 0 orders, Rs 0 revenue and Rs 0 average.
  11. Whenever the daily report shows the average, it is revenue divided by the number of card orders, rounded down, or 0 if there are no card orders. For example, Rs 1,000 revenue over 3 orders shows Rs 333.
